WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.570
JERZY FORYCIARZ: Welcome.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:11.090
My name is Jerzy Foryciarz, and
I'm a Product Manager on GKE.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:15.380
With me on the virtual stage,
Joel Meyer, Chief Architect

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.530
at OpenX, and Ivan
Gusev, Principal

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:25.040
Infrastructure Architect at
OpenX In the next 25 minutes

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.650
we will show you how OpenX
looks at performance and cost,

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.890
how to become a hero
metering and understanding

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:39.120
your utilization on GKE, how
to optimize for what you need,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.090
and then, what does
it mean in practice?

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.300
So let's get started.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:50.760
When we talk about cost
performance optimization on GKE

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.570
we mentioned two
dimensions for a reason.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.270
While the ultimate
amount of your spend

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.850
on the infrastructure depends
on the provisioned resources,

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.330
it is important to
understand your application,

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.750
how it operates,
and what you expect

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.730
from the service it represents.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:12.690
So is your application
stateless or stateful?

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.450
Is it serving or
data processing?

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.420
How long does it take to start
from deployment to active,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:22.410
and how much time
does it need to drain?

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:28.890
So a second factor, expected SLO
of service you are providing.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.030
What is the latency of the
service that you expect?

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:38.550
Can you afford longer start up
time if this means lower cost?

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:42.030
Do you need to provision
for high availability?

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.430
How does it impact the
choice of machine types

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.190
and the level of
overprovisioning?

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.440
So how do you do
this in practice?

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.240
Let's hear the life story
from Joel and Ivan from OpenX.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.410
JOEL MEYER: Thanks, Jerzy.

00:01:57.410 --> 00:01:59.840
So these numbers represent
OpenX in a nutshell

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.860
prior to our migration.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.340
We were a hundred
billion requests per day.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.300
We're running 15
million lines of code,

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.430
deployed on 15,000 servers
around the globe in five

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.790
different regions.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.250
Now OpenX is an ad exchange.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:15.750
And so those billions
of ad requests

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.790
are coming from a
multitude of clients

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.640
with an SLA of between
150 to 400 milliseconds,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.190
depending on the client.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.900
And we call the set
of services that

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.240
handle those requests
the delivery system,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.900
and that comprises about half
of our infrastructure cost.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.660
So as you can see from the load
balancer stance on the screen,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.180
we have both a daily pattern
with peaks and troughs,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.457
and then a weekly pattern
as well, with lower traffic

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:40.290
on weekends and then
higher during the week.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.690
Prior to migration, we had
pretty traditional deployment

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:47.650
strategy.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.870
Services were packaged
as RPMs and they

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:53.040
were deployed via SaltStack
on physical hardware.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.250
But we felt that being on prem
was limiting our efficiency.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:57.910
So when we moved to
the Cloud, our goal

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:00.730
was to modernize for the
purposes of gaining efficiency,

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:03.205
scalability, and consistency.

00:03:03.205 --> 00:03:05.840
And we felt that Kubernetes
was the right answer for that,

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.173
but we also knew we didn't
want to be running Kubernetes

00:03:08.173 --> 00:03:09.410
ourselves.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.320
So when we evaluated
the different offerings

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.900
from the competitors, it
seemed like GKE was probably

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.880
about two years ahead
of everybody else,

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.400
both in terms of
features and stability.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.320
Now, early on, Ivan had
looked at the CPU utilization

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.923
of all of our services,
both at peak and on average,

00:03:26.923 --> 00:03:28.340
to get a sense of
how much compute

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.230
we'd actually need in the cloud.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.340
And what he found was
pretty interesting.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.000
So the x-axis here
is peak utilization,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.140
and the y-axis is
average utilization.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.670
Now, in an on-prem environment,
your server count's fixed,

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.070
so unless your service is
running at full tilt all day

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.040
long, off peak is
basically wasted compute.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.950
And with our diurnal traffic
pattern and our weekend

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:52.440
patterns, we had a
lot of wasted compute.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.000
The graph also
illustrated for us

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.280
that we could save
significantly on compute cost

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.740
if we could buy only what
we needed throughout the day

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.630
and could share hardware
with auxiliary services.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.900
Now, keep in mind
that this is only one

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.900
dimension of CPU utilization.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.350
We're not looking
at memory or I/O.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:10.600
Those aren't captured, either.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.860
And that's why some of the
numbers may look a little sad.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:17.820
So the services circled there,
those are the delivery system.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.250
And we knew that these were
the candidates for GKE,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.830
and that moving to
GKE would enable

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:26.290
us to scale compute based on
our traffic patterns and needs,

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.160
and also to gain efficiency
by optimal packing

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.760
of the services on hardware.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.930
Let's take a brief look at
some of the before and after.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.430
So on-prem, services
were rarely restarted.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.080
You would deploy
and you would start,

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.540
and it would run unless
there was a problem.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:47.330
Also they didn't grow
and shrink in number.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.280
Whereas in the cloud, the
pods were very dynamic,

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.670
spinning up, spinning down.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:57.180
And also the cluster size was
dynamic, growing or shrinking.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.960
So we're going from a
very static environment

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.680
to a very dynamic environment.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.500
And that's what we
wanted, but it also

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.790
introduced some
challenges for us.

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.490
A few other differences
worth noting.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:12.920
On-prem, hardware is
dedicated to a service.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.690
And as a result,
that service was

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:18.350
tuned for maximum
utilization of that server,

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.150
whereas in the cloud
you're optimizing

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:23.505
your pod for the resources
that you've allocated to it.

00:05:23.505 --> 00:05:25.130
And then you're
optimizing your cluster

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:28.340
for overall utilization.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:31.410
So what that means
practically speaking is

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.910
we weren't just migrating from
one containerized environment

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:34.570
to another.

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.180
We were moving from a
very server-based model

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.670
to a completely unoptimized
container model,

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:41.458
and that added a
lot of complexity.

00:05:41.458 --> 00:05:43.000
But what it did mean
is that we could

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.795
go from using the fewest servers
possible at peak to a world

00:05:47.795 --> 00:05:50.170
where we could provision only
what we needed at any given

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:54.710
time, and that was our
definition of victory.

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:57.550
So we had an idea of what
was technically possible

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:58.750
in Kubernetes.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:01.740
But we still needed to translate
that into a cost model.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:05.260
And to accomplish that, Ivan did
some pretty extensive modeling.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.000
There were a couple
dimensions to that.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:10.570
First, he modeled the type
of compute that we would use.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.880
Would we be using on
demand, or could we

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.520
benefit from a commitment
and use committed use?

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.110
Or most cost effectively,
could we use preemptable nodes?

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:23.560
Second, he translated compute to
hosted services in some cases.

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:27.160
Rather than running
[? ReactKB ?] on PBMs,

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.670
we decided, let's take
advantage of Bigtable.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:33.130
Let's take advantage of
LB, of Load Balancing.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:34.630
And, finally, he
adjusted the amount

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:37.240
based on our actual utilization.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:40.450
So that model gave us a good
idea of what we could get to.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.570
Actually getting to
that target efficiency

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:45.400
is the journey that we'd
like to share with you.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.280
JERZY FORYCIARZ: Before we go
on the journey of autoscaling,

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:52.150
let's recap what
we're dealing with.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.700
In Kubernetes, there are
two layers of abstraction,

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:57.490
the workload layer where
you deploy your pods,

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:01.460
and the cluster layer,
where you deal with nodes.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:04.660
Google Kubernetes Engine
provides automation

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:08.470
at both of these layers so that
your service works optimally

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.190
all the time.

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:14.170
The Horizontal Pod
Autoscaler, or HPA,

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:17.620
is adding and removing
pods at the same time

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.440
based on the utilization
metrics, which

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:23.740
can be CPU utilization,
or custom metrics,

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.500
like requests per second
or number of tasks

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:28.360
in a pub/sub queue.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.110
This solves for the
cases where more requests

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:35.950
call for more machines,
which would be likely

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:40.300
the case of your serving front
end, application back end,

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:43.490
or pull off processing workers.

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:48.460
But what if the amount of
memory or CPU is not appropriate

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:53.440
for your process, or your system
cannot scale by adding more

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:54.940
machines easily?

00:07:54.940 --> 00:08:00.110
Take a database or file
system as an example.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:04.960
This is where Vertical Pod
Autoscaler comes to rescue.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:07.960
It is monitoring the
resource utilization

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:10.540
and allows for adjusting
the pod requests

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:17.460
to either improve performance
or reduce unused resources.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.950
Similarly, on the
infrastructure layer,

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:24.980
Cluster Autoscaler
automatically adds and removes

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.570
nodes in the cluster.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.080
This operation
happens in response

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:34.190
to pending pod requests
by the scheduler and CPU

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:37.880
utilization of the node.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:42.850
Node autoprovisioning decides
on the type of node pools

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:45.400
on your behalf automatically.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:48.850
This way, Cluster
Autoscaler can always

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:52.090
keep the most optimal,
meaning cheapest,

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:55.120
configuration of your cluster.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.580
So in summary, GKE is
offering four dimensions

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.120
of autoscaling to help you
optimize cost and performance

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:05.530
of your workloads.

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:12.212
So how did OpenX use them
to optimize for cost?

00:09:12.212 --> 00:09:13.420
IVAN GUSEV: Thank you, Jerzy.

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:15.840
Glad to be here.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:19.300
Horizontal Pod Autoscalers
allow each deployment

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.750
to scale independently
while maintaining each pod

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:25.720
workload at nearly concentrate.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:30.280
This significantly reduces the
number of performance scenarios

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:34.360
that need to be tested
and optimized for.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.430
We found that for
most workloads,

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:42.250
CPU utilization targets work
best as changes in code,

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:45.610
often changing
execution efficiency,

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:47.920
and would need to be
constantly adjusted

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.720
if a custom metric is used.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:53.300
As a global company,
we also [? served ?]

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:57.140
regional differences in
the coded [INAUDIBLE] pass.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:59.930
This can significantly
complicate application rating

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.510
for particular workload volumes.

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:06.870
Custom metrics are still
useful if your workload is not

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:11.550
CPU bound, but rather
I/O or network bound.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:14.900
Remember, Kubernetes does
not give you explicit choice

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:17.680
over pod division process.

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:20.470
When deployment is
scaled down, Kubernetes

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:25.280
observes pod disruption budget
while randomly selecting a pod

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:26.050
to scale down.

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:33.230
This creates imbalance
in utilization of nodes.

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:35.120
Cluster Autoscaler
normally takes

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.790
care of underutilized nodes.

00:10:37.790 --> 00:10:39.950
If you need an
additional control,

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.410
you can use the
scheduler, an open source

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:47.060
project helping to balance
resource utilization of nodes.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:52.770
When a cluster has one
or more pods ending,

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.600
the cluster Autoscaler
kicks in and determines

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:59.680
what node pools can and
should increase in size

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:02.550
to accommodate these workloads.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:05.140
To choose the best
option, it considers

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:08.110
fitness of each
node configuration

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:11.740
to accommodate pending pods.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:14.550
In other words, how well
particular node size will match

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:18.010
pods that need to be scheduled.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:21.690
It also takes into account an
estimated cost of cluster size

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:25.050
increase and will account
to increase multiple node

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:27.620
pools sequentially.

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:30.650
So some kind of [? fold ?] or
[? stock ?] out can then be

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:32.860
detected.

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:36.500
At OpenX we started with
very prescriptive model

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:39.590
when we defined exactly
what node pool will

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:42.110
host each workload.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.720
However, that often
meant that there is only

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:51.020
one application pod per node.

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:54.470
When we allow the Cluster
Autoscaler to choose the best

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:57.020
node pool dynamically,
we found that

00:11:57.020 --> 00:12:00.980
provisioned virtual machines
match our workloads much more

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:09.760
closely, which use less memory
and less CPU, [? based ?] less.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:15.610
GKE also offers fast scale down
cluster of the Scaler profile,

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:19.930
which removes underutilized
nodes much more rapidly,

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:25.520
thus lowering the cost
of your cluster faster.

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:28.410
Joel, back to you.

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:29.680
JOEL MEYER: Thanks, Ivan.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:31.750
So Ivan just
described the benefits

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:36.160
of the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler
and the Cluster Autoscaling.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:38.680
Once we had implemented
both of those,

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:41.630
our compute usage began to
follow our traffic patterns,

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:44.710
both the diurnal daily pattern
and the weekly pattern, which

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:46.720
you can see on the slide there.

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:48.600
That's the beauty
of Autoscaling.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:50.980
When it's enabled at
the application layer

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:53.200
and at the hardware
layer, it translates

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:56.120
into cost efficiency.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:59.030
Let's take a brief side journey
to talk about measurement.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:00.890
So optimization
without measurement

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:03.840
is nearly impossible.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:06.020
You need visibility
into your starting point

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:08.280
and where your resources
are being consumed.

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:11.360
So you need the ability
to measure improvement.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:13.340
You want to understand
your pod utilization,

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.200
how are they performing.

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:17.300
You want to understand
your node utilization,

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:20.300
how many are being used
and to what extent.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:22.250
You also need to understand
your overall cost,

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.375
because at the end of the
day, that's what matters.

00:13:24.375 --> 00:13:27.110
But really, you'd love
to measure everything,

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:29.210
and we used a combination
of different tools

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:30.350
to accomplish that.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:33.700
First, we used
Prometheus and Grafana

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:36.260
to measure service metrics
and application metrics.

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:38.000
All our services
were instrumented,

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:40.370
and Kubernetes offers those
metrics out of the box,

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:41.760
as well.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:44.230
Then we also used BigQuery
to track the cost metrics

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:45.722
that GCP makes available.

00:13:45.722 --> 00:13:47.180
And there's an
entire separate talk

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.890
on that topic, which we
encourage you to check out.

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:52.700
And then finally, we use
the GKE Metering Dashboard

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.290
to see which services
were consuming

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:56.720
the most resources and cost.

00:13:56.720 --> 00:13:59.720
When it comes to
optimization, your destination

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:03.160
is the model you've created,
and measurement is your GPS.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:04.910
It tells you how far
you've come and also

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:06.525
how far you've got to go.

00:14:06.525 --> 00:14:08.150
Let's talk about some
of the next stops

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:10.950
on our optimization journey.

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:11.780
IVAN GUSEV: Sure.

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:14.870
With HPA and cluster
Autoscaling configured,

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:18.770
we next looked at
pod optimization.

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:21.860
For the average case,
pod tuning is a matter

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:26.780
of making sure you understand
ratio of RAM and CPU

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:31.430
needed for the application to
operate at certain throughput.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:35.180
Then you just set requests
and limits accordingly.

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:39.020
In some cases, you may also need
to tune underlying technology,

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:42.380
like JVM, if needed.

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:45.800
If your pod isn't utilizing
the requested resources,

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:49.820
you can reduce the request,
which will release the capacity

00:14:49.820 --> 00:14:51.780
back to the cluster.

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:55.310
If your pod's consistently
over utilizing

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:58.100
the requested
resources, then you

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:00.770
may need to increase
the request in order

00:15:00.770 --> 00:15:03.430
to improve service continuity.

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:06.950
Here you see Auto
Memory Killer in action.

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:10.240
GKE provides Vertical
Pod Autoscaler

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:13.190
that watches your pod
resource utilization

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:16.400
and recommends pod sizes.

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:21.380
It is an automated way of
process that we used at OpenX.

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:25.320
JERZY FORYCIARZ: Vertical Pod
Autoscaling does three things.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:29.930
It watches the resource
usage by the selected pods.

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:33.530
After it collects enough data,
it provides recommendations

00:15:33.530 --> 00:15:37.340
to what should be the
actual resource should

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:39.650
be specified in the pod spec.

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:43.070
And then, if enabled, it
applies the recommendation

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:45.920
to selected pods.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:48.080
You can apply the
recommendations yourself,

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:52.370
similarly to what OpenX
did with their workloads.

00:15:52.370 --> 00:15:55.760
You can also enable automatic
activation of your pods

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:57.410
according to the
recommendations.

00:15:57.410 --> 00:16:01.760
This can happen in two
ways, at pod creation time

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:03.490
or continuously.

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:05.120
JOEL MEYER: The
VPA recommendations

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:07.810
that Jerzy just described
are incredibly useful.

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:09.830
And another GKE
feature that enabled

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:12.950
us to optimize performance
was CPU Manager,

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:16.202
which Ivan is going to tell
us a little bit more about.

00:16:16.202 --> 00:16:16.910
IVAN GUSEV: Sure.

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:20.630
Just like the noisy neighbor
effect on virtual machines,

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:23.570
a similar effect
exists for pods.

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:27.170
Sometimes that's by design,
to achieve overall higher

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:29.260
virtual machine utilization.

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:32.720
However, OpenX runs a large
number of applications

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:34.370
written in Erlang.

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:37.160
And Erlang virtual machine
has its own schedule

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:40.070
of threads which work
most efficiently if they

00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:44.810
run on the same CPU core all
the time and aren't swapped out

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:49.500
or competing for CPU
with other applications.

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.770
For scenarios like
this, Kubernetes

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:54.370
has a feature
called CPU Manager.

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:59.300
It provides additional
guarantees of CPU allocation

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:02.990
on top of what Kubernetes
resource requests are already

00:17:02.990 --> 00:17:04.670
doing.

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:08.960
While resource requests are only
used by Kubernetes Scheduler

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:13.339
to make important decisions
on where to schedule pods,

00:17:13.339 --> 00:17:18.810
the CPU Manager enforces
CPU allocation to the pod,

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:21.660
continuously prohibiting
all other pods

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:25.740
from stepping into CPU
managed pod territory

00:17:25.740 --> 00:17:30.000
and minimizing the number
of contact switches.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.420
The flip side of CPU Manager
is that pod cannot exceed

00:17:33.420 --> 00:17:40.000
their requests and efficiently
sandboxed in terms of CPU

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:41.960
capacity.

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:46.315
For our Erlang systems,
that's exactly what we wanted.

00:17:46.315 --> 00:17:48.310
Joel?

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:51.250
JOEL MEYER: So with Autoscaling
enabled and our pods tuned,

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:54.590
we still needed to tackle
another area of optimization--

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:56.500
how do we make the most
use of a cluster that

00:17:56.500 --> 00:17:59.290
has diverse set of
services running in it?

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:02.027
So in a nutshell, how do
we optimally bin pack?

00:18:02.027 --> 00:18:03.610
There are two parts
to that challenge.

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:05.770
One, how do we size our
pods so that they're

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:07.030
more easily accessible?

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:09.280
And then two, how
do we size our nodes

00:18:09.280 --> 00:18:12.010
so that they can accommodate
the right number of pods?

00:18:12.010 --> 00:18:14.060
We have some control
over both dimensions,

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:17.010
so we needed to
find a sweet spot.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:20.150
IVAN GUSEV: Kubernetes Scheduler
takes several pods' properties

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:23.540
into consideration to
make decision on what

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:26.140
node pod gets scheduled.

00:18:26.140 --> 00:18:28.690
Two primary capacity
related fields

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:33.460
are resource requests,
or CPU and memory.

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:37.600
Kubernetes Scheduler does
not scale cluster up or down,

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:40.870
but rather works
with available nodes,

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:44.140
aiming to fit as
many pods as possible

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:46.740
into provision capacity.

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:49.900
And when this is not
possible and pods

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:54.740
are pending, Cluster Autoscaler
provisions more nodes.

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:58.850
Here's an example of CPU and
memory constrained nodes.

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:03.740
On the left graph, you can
see that even though only 78%

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:08.930
of CPU capacity is allocated,
no pods larger than 4 CPUs

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:10.580
can be scheduled.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:14.290
Similarly, on high
CPU node scheduler,

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:20.180
node scheduler cannot add any
new pods because the single pod

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:24.780
has already allocated pretty
much all memory capacity.

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:29.120
So we ended up with CPU resource
utilization of just 28%.

00:19:32.389 --> 00:19:36.035
How can this impact
inefficiency,

00:19:36.035 --> 00:19:38.130
and can it be improved?

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:41.400
You can make all pods
equal size and make sure

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:46.260
that each node can
fit exactly n pods.

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:50.640
This is not always ideal, as
workload capacity requirements

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:55.030
and ability to scale
vertically differ a lot.

00:19:55.030 --> 00:19:59.190
At OpenX, we use 2 to
the power of n rule.

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:04.780
We standardize pod sizes
on 1, 2, 4, and 16 cores.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:09.040
This also matches
GCE standard VM sizes

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:12.040
and allows us to move
between different VM sizes

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:16.800
without a need to
optimize pod sizes again.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:21.300
While doing so, make sure that
you account for space reserve

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:25.440
by Kubernetes for
daemon sets and kernel.

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:28.290
Also don't forget to pay
attention to your sidecar

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:33.330
and init containers, as their
resource requests might be

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:36.520
affecting scheduling decisions.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:39.070
Generally speaking, you
will get best results

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:43.900
of bin packing when pod requests
are small fractions of CPUs

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:46.880
while nodes are
relatively large.

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:50.290
Yet if you're using
preemptable virtual machines,

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:53.650
you have to watch for losing
a large number of pods

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:55.164
in case of preemption.

00:20:57.770 --> 00:20:59.870
Measuring the bin
packing efficiency

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:04.280
is quite simple, as you need to
aggregate all workflow resource

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:08.530
requests and divide
it by total capacity.

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:12.280
It can be tracked as a cluster
wide metric, or node pool

00:21:12.280 --> 00:21:14.370
specific ratio.

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:18.780
We observe that Autoscaled
clusters with no pod size

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:24.960
optimizations we usually get
around 75% of CPU reserve,

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:27.840
while clusters with improved
bin packing efficiency

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:31.130
reach all the way to 95%.

00:21:31.130 --> 00:21:34.800
Similar optimizations can be
done for memory dimension.

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:40.700
However, memory generally
requires significant headroom,

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:44.670
and usage is less predictable
compared to CPU cores.

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:47.420
Our memory reservations
are currently

00:21:47.420 --> 00:21:50.840
measured at around 50%.

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:54.680
Optimization of bin packing
in more than one dimension

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:57.800
is totally possible, but
depending on your workload

00:21:57.800 --> 00:22:00.950
profile, it may end up
exponentially harder

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:05.430
to implement with every
new dimension that you add.

00:22:05.430 --> 00:22:09.082
We decided to leave this
exercise for the future.

00:22:09.082 --> 00:22:09.582
Joel?

00:22:09.582 --> 00:22:11.420
JOEL MEYER: Ivan.

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:14.570
So early on when we were
doing our Cloud cost modeling,

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:16.160
Ivan picked out
services that could

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:19.140
take advantage of
preemptable virtual machines.

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:22.160
And for the delivery stack,
we assume that roughly 70%

00:22:22.160 --> 00:22:24.830
of our compute could
be preemptable Now

00:22:24.830 --> 00:22:26.930
when we first tested,
we were discouraged

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:30.290
because it was hard to get
the PBM capacity we needed.

00:22:30.290 --> 00:22:32.510
But after discussing
with the GCE team,

00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:35.960
they encouraged us to try
a smaller sized PBM node.

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:38.330
After making that change,
we were much more successful

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:40.850
in finding the PBM
capacity, and we have even

00:22:40.850 --> 00:22:44.540
exceeded our initial
target PBM utilization.

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:47.720
But it is another factor
to consider in bin packing.

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:50.040
So the GKE cluster
autoscaler allows

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:51.703
us to tap into
different PBM pools

00:22:51.703 --> 00:22:53.370
by attempting to scale
up different node

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:54.490
pools sequentially.

00:22:54.490 --> 00:22:56.610
That could be node pools
of different VM sizes,

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:58.635
or using different
compute families.

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:04.550
So while we've
presented our journey

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:06.630
as a nice linear
progression, the reality

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:08.630
was a bit more winding,
with some back and forth

00:23:08.630 --> 00:23:11.210
between the different
optimization techniques.

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:14.330
That being said, the six
approaches we've discussed

00:23:14.330 --> 00:23:15.620
are all worth looking into.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:19.430
First, configure and tune your
Horizontal Pod Autoscalers.

00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:22.260
Two, enable the
Cluster Autoscaler.

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:23.930
Three, rightsize
your pod requests

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:27.510
and/or use the Vertical
Pod Autoscaler.

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:30.840
Number four, use the CPU Manager
for the applications that

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:33.570
need that level of guarantee.

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:35.848
Number five, optimize
your bin packing.

00:23:35.848 --> 00:23:37.890
And then, number six, find
the right machine type

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:39.730
for your workload.

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:43.220
So thanks to the work that
Ivan and the team have done,

00:23:43.220 --> 00:23:45.160
we're now able to
run our GKE clusters

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:46.720
on a very small
set of node pools

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:49.540
that scale based on
price and availability.

00:23:49.540 --> 00:23:51.190
And thanks to the
optimized pods,

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:54.250
the HPAs, the right
choices in machine sizes,

00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:58.170
we've improved
efficiency by over 45%.

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:00.720
One of the things that was
invaluable to the success

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:03.270
of this project was the
ability to meet with and ask

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:07.780
questions of the different teams
whose technology we're using.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:10.920
Whether that was Jerzy and
the GKE Autoscalers Team,

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:13.530
the CPU Manager
Team, the GCE Team,

00:24:13.530 --> 00:24:16.755
or any one of the other
teams that we met with,

00:24:16.755 --> 00:24:18.630
we were really impressed
by their willingness

00:24:18.630 --> 00:24:22.330
to work with us and help
us use their technology.

00:24:22.330 --> 00:24:24.690
We also took advantage
of the GCP PSO team

00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:27.300
to help us in various
parts of the migration

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:29.490
and found their help to
be very valuable, as well.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:32.475
Thank you, particularly,
to Will [? Beebee. ?]

00:24:32.475 --> 00:24:35.360
So Jerzy, thanks for allowing
us to share some of our story

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:36.350
here with you.

00:24:36.350 --> 00:24:38.210
And to the audience,
good luck with your own

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:39.910
optimization efforts.

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:41.160
Jerzy, back to you.

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:42.910
JERZY FORYCIARZ: Thank
you, Joel and Ivan,

00:24:42.910 --> 00:24:46.110
for sharing your
experience with us.

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:50.105
For those of you willing to get
on a journey of optimizing cost

00:24:50.105 --> 00:24:54.560
to performance on GKE, we just
published two solution guides

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:57.830
that will get you an advice
on best practices of running

00:24:57.830 --> 00:25:00.650
cost effective
Kubernetes applications

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:04.760
and how to use node
autoprovisioning

00:25:04.760 --> 00:25:07.500
to optimize your resources.

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:11.870
These guides are a collective
summary of experiences of OpenX

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:16.420
and many other companies who
shared their stories with us.

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:18.910
We will keep
updating it as we add

00:25:18.910 --> 00:25:23.600
more functionality to
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:27.800
You should also take a look
at related breakout sessions

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:30.850
airing this week.

00:25:30.850 --> 00:25:33.390
On behalf of Google
Cloud Team, I

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:36.150
want to thank you for watching.

